he error mode and pipe status, marking failed outputs and continuing to read from stdin until EOF, then returning an error code if any failures occurred.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the default mode behavior—requirement R4 specifies that without the flag, tee should diagnose errors on non-pipe outputs, continue processing, and exit with code 1 at the end if any errors happened. This means the default mode is essentially \"warn-nopipe\" but with a final exit code, distinct from \"warn\" mode which continues without setting an exit code. I'm getting confused about the exact behavior of each mode, so let me just implement something reasonable and let the differential tests validate it against GNU tee. For the diagnostic behavior, I'll have default and warn-nopipe report non-pipe errors, warn report all errors, and exit modes report based on whether it's a pipe. For the exit behavior, I need to figure out which modes should actually cause the process to stop versus just skip that output. I need to handle the `--version` flag to output the version information in a standard format, and properly format error messages to match GNU tee's style of showing just the path and error description. I also need to be careful about file closing in loops and make sure to handle the special case where `-` represents stdout as a file argument.\n\nLooking at the test case with `-` as a file argument, I'm realizing that GNU tee treats `-` as `/dev/stdout`, which means it writes to stdout in addition to the regular stdout output. The test description says \"output is not duplicated,\" but that's actually how GNU tee behaves—it's a differential test comparing both binaries against each other rather than against fixed expected values, so I should just match GNU's actual behavior.\n\nNow I need to implement the core logic, starting with a helper function to detect whether a file descriptor is a pipe using syscall operations.\n\nWriting the implementation...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"s